--
N5008 [over.match.class.deduct]/5, [over.match.copy]/1.1: the notional
constructor formed from an explicit constructor keeps its explicit-specifier,
and in copy-initialization only converting constructors are candidates, so
`E e = 1;' has no viable deduction guide and the program is ill-formed.  The
front end deduced E<int> anyway (through the positional fallback) and then
rejected the initialization by dropping main's body ("could not fully
type-check 'main'", after ef2a71f448).  deduce_class_template_arguments now
skips explicit constructors for `C x = e' and fails the deduction ("found
no match for symbol 'E'", as on bin53, which passes this test).
test_converting.desc: the guide from a converting constructor with default
arguments is chosen ([over.match.viable]/2.3; failed on bin53).  g++ 13 and
clang++ 18 reject this program.
